{
  "teamId": 108,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Los Angeles Angels entered the season with cautious optimism, buoyed by the return of Shohei Ohtani from Tommy John surgery and the continued brilliance of Mike Trout. But from the very first week, the cracks in the pitching staff were evident — a 5.12 ERA would plague the club all year, and the bullpen in particular became a recurring source of heartbreak. The Angels managed to keep their heads above water through the spring and early summer, posting winning records in May, June, and July, riding an offense that slugged 220 home runs and moments of genuine heroism from unlikely sources like Brian Goodwin and Kole Calhoun.\n\nThe summer months told a story of a team that could not get out of its own way. For every Pujols clutch knock or Ohtani flash of brilliance, Hansel Robles or Cam Bedrosian would surrender a crushing blow in a high-leverage moment. The bullpen's inability to protect leads cost the Angels game after game, and the month-by-month records began to reflect the toll — a respectable 14-11 in July gave way to a catastrophic 9-18 in August, effectively ending any lingering playoff hope.\n\nAugust was where the season died. Albert Pujols provided one of the year's most emotional moments with a walk-off single against Boston on August 30th, but that joy was quickly swallowed by the broader collapse. Errors, blown saves, and a Rangers series that featured multiple gut-punch defeats underscored how thin the margin was between this team's offensive ceiling and its pitching floor. By the time September arrived, the Angels were playing out the string, finishing 7-18 in the final month.\n\nIn the end, the 2019 Angels were a study in the limits of individual greatness. Mike Trout won his third MVP award and was arguably the best player on the planet, yet the team finished 72-90. Ohtani showed flashes of his two-way potential as a hitter recovering from surgery. But without a competent rotation or a reliable bullpen, no amount of offensive firepower — not even 769 runs — could overcome a pitching staff that surrendered 868. It was a lost season, and the Angels organization was left to confront hard questions about how long they could afford to waste the best years of their generational talent.",
  "chapters": [
